ad","steps":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Mix all of the dry ingredients then add the butter, honey and sourdough starter.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ephhs5435","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-43iv7","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Slowly add a cup of the warm whey while the mixer is running on low speed until the dough begins to come together adding more if you need it a tablespoon at a time. You can add a bit more flour if you go over and it gets too sticky. Let the mixer knead the dough 7-8 minutes more.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"00cj25437","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-1lg58","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"9mrwo5438","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4r9r6","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Now scrape the dough out of the mixer and onto a floured board. Knead the dough for a minute or two then shape it into a ball. Roll it around in a large bowl with a little good quality oil to coat it and cover it with plastic wrap to rise for about 1 1/2 hours.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"f7st85440","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-d1ajd","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"9ieia5441","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bn8f0","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"After the dough has risen to about double in size you can form your loaf by kneading a few times then rolling the dough up like a newspaper and pinching closed the seem. See the illustrations above for more tips on forming your loaf.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"8mhw25443","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-8jqkj","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"duz805444","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-dncel","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Let the dough rise in the loaf pan covered loosely with plastic wrap that has been sprayed with oil for about 30 minutes to 1 hour while preheating your oven to 375 degrees.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"tlhvn5446","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-1o0u2","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"zdu7h5447","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-evatq","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"After the dough has risen 1/2 inch or so above the rim of your loaf pan, remove the plastic wrap, gently brush the top with melted butter and then put it on the middle rack in the preheated oven for 25-30 minutes. It will rise even more in the oven so be sure it's not too close to the top element. Turn the loaf front to back halfway through to ensure even baking and color. When it's done it will sound quite hollow on top when tapped.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"nidz75449","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4g0t","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"cfyaq5450","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-460ch","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Brush it again with melted butter and let it cool for about 10 minutes before loosening it from the pan and cooling it on a wire rack. Let cool at least 45 minutes before cutting.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:34:58.540Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:34:58.540Z","id":"d501dd73-be2b-4d81-a7b7-1853984cbcc6"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"Mix all of the dry ingredients then add the butter, honey and sourdough starter.\n\nSlowly add a cup of the warm whey while the mixer is running on low speed until the dough begins to come together adding more if you need it a tablespoon at a time. You can add a bit more flour if you go over and it gets too sticky. Let the mixer knead the dough 7-8 minutes more.\n\n \n\nNow scrape the dough out of the mixer and onto a floured board. Knead the dough for a minute or two then shape it into a ball. Roll it around in a large bowl with a little good quality oil to coat it and cover it with plastic wrap to rise for about 1 1/2 hours.\n\n \n\nAfter the dough has risen to about double in size you can form your loaf by kneading a few times then rolling the dough up like a newspaper and pinching closed the seem. See the illustrations above for more tips on forming your loaf.\n\n \n\nLet the dough rise in the loaf pan covered loosely with plastic wrap that has been sprayed with oil for about 30 minutes to 1 hour while preheating your oven to 375 degrees.\n\n \n\nAfter the dough has risen 1/2 inch or so above the rim of your loaf pan, remove the plastic wrap, gently brush the top with melted butter and then put it on the middle rack in the preheated oven for 25-30 minutes. It will rise even more in the oven so be sure it's not too close to the top element. Turn the loaf front to back halfway through to ensure even baking and color. When it's done it will sound quite hollow on top when tapped.\n\n \n\nBrush it again with melted butter and let it cool for about 10 minutes before loosening it from the pan and cooling it on a wire rack. Let cool at least 45 minutes before cutting.","title":"Soft Sprouted Wheat Multigrain Sourdough Sandwich Bread"},{"introduction":"Jumbo Apple Granola Sourdough Breakfast Muffins are hearty and tasty! They will fill you up and keep you going. The granola softens as it soaks up the moisture from the apples and the maple syrup giving these sourdough muffins great texture and flavor that's not too dense.","servings":"6 Jumbo Muffins","photo":"wix:image://v1/577c92_44dc020c79224e73aad753a8acbeb470~mv2_d_3000_2250_s_2.jpg/20191016_093830_edited.jpg#