foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-17T23:26:52.736Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-17T23:26:52.736Z","id":"cd55ac0a-7ee0-4db8-b2d5-25d4a4142480"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"Start preheating an oven to 400 degrees with a large cast iron skillet inside.\n\n​\n\nMix together all of the dry ingredients including 1/4 cup shredded cheese and the diced jalapenos in one bowl.\n\n​\n\nAdd the wet ingredients to the dry ingredients and stir just enough to moisten the dry ingredients. \n\n​\n\nRemove the pre heated skillet from the oven and add 1 tablespoon butter. Swirl to melt and coat the pan.\n\n​\n\nPour the batter into the buttered skillet and place it in the oven.\n\n​\n\nAfter 15 minutes, remove the 1/2 cooked corn bread from the oven and top with the rest of the cheese and the sliced jalapenos.\n\n \n\nContinue to bake the cornbread another 15 minutes or until the top is golden brown and a tooth pick inserted comes out clean.\n\n​\n\nLet cool 5 minutes or more and serve.","title":"Sweet and Crispy Jalapeno Cornbread Skillet"},{"introduction":"I always have bits of leftover bread ends and pieces after dinner and this is what I do with them.\nI save all of the ends and pieces in my freezer until I have enough for a batch of oven baked croutons. The more varieties of breads involved the better this is and these crunchy savory croutons are so easy to make, just thaw out the bread pieces and get chopping.","servings":"variable","photo":"image://v1/577c92_bb41baab347d4ade95cf6b37b0739023~mv2_d_3024_4032_s_4_2.jpg/3024_4032/20170302_164200.jpg","link-recipes-1-title":"/bread-and-sourdough/-how-to-make-great-croutons","url":"http://www.theglasspantry.com/single-post/2017/03/06/How-to-Make-Great-Croutons","image":"wix:image://v1/577c92_bb41baab347d4ade95cf6b37b0739023~mv2_d_3024_4032_s_4_2.jpg/20170302_164200.jpg#originWidth=3024&originHeight=4032","_id":"9df18986-43f3-452f-96bf-87ecf565df8b","_owner":"577c92d8-55f7-4f98-88a2-d8647b1c2c9c","_createdDate":{"$date":"2023-02-17T18:37:31.237Z"},"title1":" How to Make Great Croutons","shortDescription":"\nI always have bits of leftover bread ends and pieces after dinner and this is what I do with them.\nI save all of the ends and pieces in my freezer until I have enough for a batch of oven baked croutons. The more varieties of breads involved the better this is and these crunchy savory croutons are so easy to make, just thaw out the bread pieces and get chopping.","link-recipes-1-all":"/recipes-1/","cookingTime":"30 minutes","_updatedDate":{"$date":"2023-03-08T23:56:49.120Z"},"recipesCatagoryCatagory":"/Recipes/catagory/Bread","preparationTime":"10 minutes","ingredients":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Lots of leftover bread cut into cubes or torn in pieces (about 4-5 cups)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"dnad41107","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-3k207","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Seasoned salt & pepper mix of your choice to taste","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"3o3b31109","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-2jpv4","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Approximately 1/4 cup Extra Virgin Olive Oil","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:59:31.308Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:59:31.308Z","id":"8ca7cfc5-4ad2-4041-b667-97664a37acf3"},"documentStyle":{}},"catagory":"Bread","steps":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Toss the bread cubes on sheet pans with the seasoned salt and then the EVOO and bake at 350 degrees for 30 minutes tossing croutons and rotating pans halfway through.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"rgb(255, 255, 255)","foreground":"rgb(28, 28, 28)"}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:59:31.479Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:59:31.479Z","id":"125e202b-e474-4af7-ab45-832e370c63ac"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"Toss the bread cubes on sheet pans with the seasoned salt and then the EVOO and bake at 350 degrees for 30 minutes tossing croutons and rotating pans halfway through.","title":" How to Make Great Croutons"},{"introduction":"This savory sourdough bread is studded with tangy sweet dried cranberries that re-hydrate during the rising and baking process. It is amazing when warmed or toasted with a bit of butter. Cranberry sourdough bread is truly a versatile bread that I like to serve with dinner or breakfast. I'm sure it's going to make fantastic grilled cheese sandwiches one day at my house. It would also be perfect for making crostini to serve with baked brie.","servings":"10-12","photo":"image://v1/577c92_a87976761d064a16acd97b4734723fe2~mv2.jpg/1500_1125/20171213_194807_edited.jpg","link-recipes-1-title":"/bread-and-sourdough/cranberry-sourdough-bread","url":"http://www.theglasspantry.com/single-post/2018/02/20/Cranberry-Sourdough-Bread","image":"wix:image://v1/577c92_dd57043651064cd290f3a258c67b9d10~mv2_d_4032_3024_s_4_2.jpg/20171230_160451.jpg#originWidth=4032&originHeight=3024","_id":"a7c4ac2f-5379-47c8-a13a-ff200feafbf8","_owner":"577c92d8-55f7-4f98-88a2-d8647b1c2c9c","_createdDate":{"$date":"2023-02-17T18:37:31.248Z"},"title1":"Cranberry Sourdough Bread","shortDescription":" This savory sourdough bread is studded with tangy sweet dried cranberries that re-hydrate during the rising and baking process. It is amazing when warmed or toasted with a bit of butter. Cranberry sourdough bread is truly a versatile bread that I like to serve with dinner or breakfast. I'm sure it's going to make fantastic grilled cheese sandwiches one day at my house. It would also be perfect for making crostini to serve with baked brie.","link-recipes-1-all":"/recipes-1/","cookingTime":"40 minutes","_updatedDate":{"$date":"2023-02-17T23:15:03.214Z"},"recipesCatagoryCatagory":"/Recipes/catagory/Bread","preparationTime":"3 hours","ingredients":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"2 1/2 cups All Purpose Flour","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"nl3et3941","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-5hp91","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3/4 cup bread flour","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"v9imo3943","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-akblq","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 teaspoon active dry yeast","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"o6o5d3945","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-5o9ed","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 1/2 tsp fine sea salt","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"eejg03947","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-duj5a","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 cup sourdough starter","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"wyzfe3949","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-65jap","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 cup very warm water or whey (100-110 degrees)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mpzyj3951","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-60fsd","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1/2 cup dried cranberries","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"0y7mt3953","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-a259t","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1/4 cup tablespoons pumpkin seeds","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"bxkew3955","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-50plf","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 1/2 tablespoons flax seeds","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"80q3m3957","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-etdsq","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1 1/2 tablespoons millet seeds","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-17T22:21:07.946Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-17T22:21:07.946Z","id":"4c48414a-740e-40c0-a389-fbda9ef9a3fd"},"documentStyle":{}},"catagory":"Bread","steps":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"(40 minutes active 2 1/2 to 3 hours total)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"q5ifm5490","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-o2nh","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"oc2do5491","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-2r2t6","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1. Add dry ingredients to a stand mixer ( I don't sift) and mix on low speed with the hook attachment to somewhat combine.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"m7gjo5493","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-7ioma","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mf98n5494","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6f80u","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"2. Add sourdough starter all at once and mix again on low speed to somewhat combine.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"2vhag5496","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4aj6","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"uicmc5497","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-9ck65","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3. Begin to drizzle in the liquid very slowly with the machine running on low speed until you have used about 1 cup.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"hojna5499","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-8ou9i","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"w2b9a5500","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-dnng","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"4. Allow the machine to continue to work the dough until it begins to pull away from the sides of the mixer adding more liquid tablespoon by tablespoon if it seems too dry. If you go too fast and the dough gets too wet add more bread flour a few tablespoons at a time. The dough should become just slightly sticky and be very soft in about 5 minutes. Let the machine knead a few minutes after it comes together about 7-8 minutes total. The dough should be very soft and not too sticky.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"awdvm5502","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-c1nsu","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"24xhs5503","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-du4aa","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"5. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula. Flour your hands and then knead the dough just a few times by folding it over itself one direction and then the other.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"jewh45505","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-e8pdh","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"eq10b5506","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4p9jo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"6. Gather the dough into a ball and place it in a large bowl that has a teaspoon of oil in it and roll it around so it's lightly covered with the oil. I use grape seed oil but any good oil will do.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"j6hsb5508","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-3aki8","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"zm8jr5509","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-2t4cg","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"7. Cover the bowl loosely with plastic wrap and set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"y16ge5511","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-63kjo","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"7ai0y5512","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-55h2g","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"vwep35514","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-e9hgt","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"9. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula and with floured hands pat it out into a disc then top it with the dried cranberries and seeds. Stretch and fold the dough over itself then turn the dough and stretch and fold again. Repeat until all of the cranberries have been incorporated into the dough then roll the bread into a ball creating surface tension as you roll until you have created a round shape. Place the dough onto a floured banneton or a bowl that you have lined with a towel and floured well. I cover this with a cotton kitchen towel or banneton cover and set it back in my warm rising space on top of my stove with the range hood lights on for a little extra warmth.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"fwp9z5566","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-efu70","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"4ni385567","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-19tlh","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"10. Put a cast iron Dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"uno405569","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-39gsb","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ur84j5570","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6fg48","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"11. After 40 to 60 minutes the dough should have nearly doubled in size so prep for scoring by putting a piece of pop-up parchment paper on a bread board and sprinkle it with cornmeal, polenta grits or semolina flour.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"7klxg5572","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-94hj7","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"td4ji5573","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-cncrm","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"12. Very carefully remove the preheated Dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ftut25575","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-e41dj","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"1fd9c5576","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-2gejg","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"13. Gently roll/flip the formed loaf out of the proofing basket onto the parchment paper sprinkled with cornmeal and then quickly score the loaf with a razor blade making three 1/2 inch deep slashes.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mqy855578","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-sqav","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"rgcx05579","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-birj7","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"14. Remove the lid from the Dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole Dutch oven back into your oven.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"fkm5z5581","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-1btab","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"10mas5582","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-8kdjc","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 degrees at this point and set a timer for 45 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40-45 minutes.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"pqqcz5584","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-5sit9","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mklbv5585","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-cal3k","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom. Let cool completely or at least an hour before slicing.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-17T22:21:46.569Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-17T22:21:46.569Z","id":"23072357-d49b-4629-9153-c3ffc1e2e9f6"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"(40 minutes active 2 1/2 to 3 hours total)\n\n1. Add dry ingredients to a stand mixer ( I don't sift) and mix on low speed with the hook attachment to somewhat combine.\n\n2. Add sourdough starter all at once and mix again on low speed to somewhat combine.\n\n3. Begin to drizzle in the liquid very slowly with the machine running on low speed until you have used about 1 cup.\n\n4. Allow the machine to continue to work the dough until it begins to pull away from the sides of the mixer adding more liquid tablespoon by tablespoon if it seems too dry. If you go too fast and the dough gets too wet add more bread flour a few tablespoons at a time. The dough should become just slightly sticky and be very soft in about 5 minutes. Let the machine knead a few minutes after it comes together about 7-8 minutes total. The dough should be very soft and not too sticky.\n\n5. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula. Flour your hands and then knead the dough just a few times by folding it over itself one direction and then the other.\n\n6. Gather the dough into a ball and place it in a large bowl that has a teaspoon of oil in it and roll it around so it's lightly covered with the oil. I use grape seed oil but any good oil will do.\n\n7. Cover the bowl loosely with plastic wrap and set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!\n\n8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!\n\n9. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula and with floured hands pat it out into a disc then top it with the dried cranberries and seeds. Stretch and fold the dough over itself then turn the dough and stretch and fold again. Repeat until all of the cranberries have been incorporated into the dough then roll the bread into a ball creating surface tension as you roll until you have created a round shape. Place the dough onto a floured banneton or a bowl that you have lined with a towel and floured well. I cover this with a cotton kitchen towel or banneton cover and set it back in my warm rising space on top of my stove with the range hood lights on for a little extra warmth.\n\n10. Put a cast iron Dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes.\n\n11. After 40 to 60 minutes the dough should have nearly doubled in size so prep for scoring by putting a piece of pop-up parchment paper on a bread board and sprinkle it with cornmeal, polenta grits or semolina flour.\n\n12. Very carefully remove the preheated Dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.\n\n13. Gently roll/flip the formed loaf out of the proofing basket onto the parchment paper sprinkled with cornmeal and then quickly score the loaf with a razor blade making three 1/2 inch deep slashes.\n\n14. Remove the lid from the Dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole Dutch oven back into your oven.\n\n15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 degrees at this point and set a timer for 45 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40-45 minutes.\n\nBread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom. Let cool completely or at least an hour before slicing.","title":"Cranberry Sourdough Bread"},{"introduction":"A fantastic healthy bread for sandwiches that tastes great cold, grilled or toasted. It's substantial enough to hold up to heavier sandwich toppings and is loaded with protein and omega 3's. ","servings":"10-12","photo":"wix:image://v1/577c92_4264ca4e653e49f2b6a80cf80827d1db~mv2_d_4032_3024_s_4_2.jpg/20190514_200141.jpg#originWidth=4032&originHeight=3024","link-recipes-1-title":"/bread-and-sourdough/sourdough-sandwich-bread-with-ground-flax-and-almonds","url":"https://www.theglasspantry.com/sourdough-sandwich-bread-flax-almon","_id":"ad7b3ba3-b4b8-436b-8158-276a03a6929b","_owner":"577c92d8-55f7-4f98-88a2-d8647b1c2c9c","_createdDate":{"$date":"2023-02-17T18:37:31.246Z"},"title1":"Sourdough Sandwich Bread with ground flax and almonds","shortDescription":"A f