beaten egg.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"lplfr4631","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"​","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"xp1ug4633","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Slowly add a cup of the warm whey while the mixer is running on low speed until the dough begins to come together adding more if you need it a tablespoon at a time. You can add a bit more flour if you go over and it gets too sticky. Let the mixer knead the dough 7-8 minutes more.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"hxgpv4635","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mre2o4637","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Now scrape the dough out of the mixer and onto a floured board. Knead the dough for a minute or two then shape it into a ball. Roll it around in a large bowl with a little good quality oil to coat it and cover it with plastic wrap to rise for about 1 1/2 hours.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"txhke4644","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"w19aw4650","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"After the dough has risen to approximately double in size you can form your loaf by kneading a few times then rolling the dough up like a newspaper and pinching closed the seem. Check out my tutorial for ","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}},{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Soft Sprouted Wheat Multigrain Sourdough Sandwich Bread","decorations":[{"type":"LINK","linkData":{"link":{"url":"https://www.theglasspantry.com/single-post/2017/03/15/Soft-Sprouted-Wheat-Multigrain-Sourdough-Sandwich-Bread","target":"BLANK"}}},{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}},{"type":"BOLD","fontWeightValue":700.0},{"type":"UNDERLINE"}]}},{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" to learn more about rising and forming the loaf.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ropvr4693","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"3qoyf4695","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Let the dough rise in the loaf pan covered loosely with plastic wrap that has been sprayed with oil for about 30 minutes to 1 hour while preheating your oven to 375 degrees.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"tm4q54697","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"bmvt44699","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"After the dough has risen 1/2 inch or so above the rim of your loaf pan, remove the plastic wrap, gently brush the top with melted butter, sprinkle the top with cornmeal and then put it on the middle rack in the preheated oven for 25-30 minutes. It will rise even more in the oven so be sure it's not too close to the top element. Turn the loaf front to back halfway through to ensure even baking and color. When it's done it will sound quite hollow on top when tapped.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"glmmp4713","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"a41qy4726","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Brush it again with melted butter and let it cool for about 10 minutes before loosening it from the pan and cooling it on a wire rack. Let cool at least 45 minutes before slicing.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"","foreground":"rgb(28, 28, 28)"}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:40:51.987Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T21:40:51.987Z","id":"ac2df766-97db-4b6a-af4e-0235fd748b01"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"Mix all of the dry ingredients then add the vegetable oil, yogurt and beaten egg.\n\nSlowly add a cup of the warm whey while the mixer is running on low speed until the dough begins to come together adding more if you need it a tablespoon at a time. You can add a bit more flour if you go over and it gets too sticky. Let the mixer knead the dough 7-8 minutes more.\n\nNow scrape the dough out of the mixer and onto a floured board. Knead the dough for a minute or two then shape it into a ball. Roll it around in a large bowl with a little good quality oil to coat it and cover it with plastic wrap to rise for about 1 1/2 hours.\n\nAfter the dough has risen to approximately double in size you can form your loaf by kneading a few times then rolling the dough up like a newspaper and pinching closed the seem. Check out my tutorial for Soft Sprouted Wheat Multigrain Sourdough Sandwich Bread to learn more about rising and forming the loaf.\n\nLet the dough rise in the loaf pan covered loosely with plastic wrap that has been sprayed with oil for about 30 minutes to 1 hour while preheating your oven to 375 degrees.\n\nAfter the dough has risen 1/2 inch or so above the rim of your loaf pan, remove the plastic wrap, gently brush the top with melted butter, sprinkle the top with cornmeal and then put it on the middle rack in the preheated oven for 25-30 minutes. It will rise even more in the oven so be sure it's not too close to the top element. Turn the loaf front to back halfway through to ensure even baking and color. When it's done it will sound quite hollow on top when tapped.\n\nBrush it again with melted butter and let it cool for about 10 minutes before loosening it from the pan and cooling it on a wire rack. Let cool at least 45 minutes before slicing.","title":"Greek Yogurt Bread"},{"introduction":" This is a lightly sweet and hearty hazelnut studded seed bread that makes wonderful breakfast toast and a great addition to any meal. It has molasses and honey and just a light tang from the sourdough. ","servings":"10-12","photo":"image://v1/577c92_b50f3aa69b8b4e98aaff38b92a7f92f5~mv2_d_4003_2676_s_4_2.jpg/4003_2676/20171019_174718.jpg","link-recipes-1-title":"/bread-and-sourdough/hazelnut-molasses-sourdough-bread","url":"http://www.theglasspantry.com/single-post/2017/11/05/Hazelnut-Molasses-Sourdough-Bread","image":"wix:image://v1/577c92_b50f3aa69b8b4e98aaff38b92a7f92f5~mv2_d_4003_2676_s_4_2.jpg/20171019_174718.jpg#originWidth=4003&originHeight=2676","_id":"d1fb0f40-181e-41ad-864d-28fd5036f6cb","_owner":"577c92d8-55f7-4f98-88a2-d8647b1c2c9c","_createdDate":{"$date":"2023-02-17T18:37:31.236Z"},"title1":"Hazelnut Molasses Sourdough Bread","shortDescription":" This is a lightly sweet and hearty hazelnut studded seed bread that makes wonderful breakfast toast and a great addition to any meal. It has molasses and honey and just a light tang from the sourdough. ","link-recipes-1-all":"/recipes-1/","cookingTime":"40 minutes","_updatedDate":{"$date":"2023-02-18T22:15:15.972Z"},"recipesCatagoryCatagory":"/Recipes/catagory/Bread","preparationTime":"3 hours","ingredients":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3 cups all-purpose flour 1/2 whole wheat flour","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"n5ayi1318","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4s81g","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1/2 bread flour 1 1/2 tsp active dry yeast 1 1/2 tsp salt 2 Tbsp Molasses 1 tablespoons Honey","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"dy9o51320","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-2pekl","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"2 tablespoons butter","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"5xxq61322","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4etog","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3 tablespoons flax seeds","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"045d41324","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-ca7jl","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3 tablespoons Millet","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"bmb6m1326","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-12k1f","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"2 tablespoons roasted unsalted sunflower seeds","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ua80f1328","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-62apd","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1/4 cup coarsely chopped hazelnuts","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ytsga1330","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bonij","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3/4 cup sourdough starter 1 to 1 1/4 cup warm water ( about 110 degrees)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"hqi271332","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-5ab5n","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"(start with 1 cup and add a tablespoon at a time until the dough comes together)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"iwmfl1334","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-5c501","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"u2mnc1335","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bmho","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Olive Oil","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"pvdpo1337","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6unpc","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Cornmeal or Semolina Flour","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:16.984Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:16.984Z","id":"851948e6-6311-483a-a7bb-c098cccda4a8"},"documentStyle":{}},"catagory":"Bread","steps":{"nodes":[{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"foo","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"1. Add the flours, butter, salt and yeast to a stand mixer ( I don't sift) and mix on low speed with the hook attachment to somewhat combine.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"},"indentation":0.0}},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"wclo61413","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-52j28","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"lf4421414","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-cgj9u","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"2. Add the molasses, honey and sourdough starter all at once and mix again on low speed to somewhat combine.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ncohl1416","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-hvnt","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"qdvk11417","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-7o04n","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"3. Begin to drizzle in the liquid very slowly with the machine running on low speed until you have used a little more than 1 cup.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mi5la1419","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-b827v","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"nsm671420","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bg53s","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"4. Allow the machine to continue to work the dough until it begins to pull away from the sides of the mixer adding more liquid tablespoon by tablespoon if it seems too dry. If you go too fast and the dough gets too wet add more bread flour a few tablespoons at a time. The dough should become just slightly sticky and be very soft in about 5 minutes. Let the machine knead a few minutes after it comes together maybe 7-8 minutes total. The dough should be just tacky now and still very soft.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"27w471422","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-9bos5","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"fnajr1423","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-71cfc","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"5. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula. Flour your hands and then knead the dough just a few times by folding it over itself one direction and then the other.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"b8dyg1425","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-dguta","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"15me61426","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4es9h","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"6. Gather the dough into a ball and place it in a large bowl that has a tablespoon of oil in it and roll it around so it's lightly covered with the oil.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"0lttz1428","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-aq19c","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"efxv71429","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bcdvs","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"7. Cover the bowl with plastic wrap and set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"zt0oi1431","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4gvkk","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"rymjm1432","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-dua2i","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"cgh4d1434","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4f4ml","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"rdw441435","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-3b9r7","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"9. Roll it back out onto a floured board and degassed the dough by folding it over on itself 3-4 times, then started rolling it up like a newspaper fairly tightly. You want to get some decent surface tension going on the outside of the loaf you are forming.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"8v66r1437","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-68dh8","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"u2kmg1438","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6kb44","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"10. I put it in a floured proofing basket called a banneton to do its second rise. Cover it with the cover that often comes with your banneton or very loosely with plastic wrap that you've sprayed with oil.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"tx08m1440","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-9jo6d","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"k1ec81441","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-47q0d","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Tip - You can make a proofing basket out of a bread basket or pan lined with towels but it's not nearly as convenient if you make bread regularly.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"4sty51443","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-39ugd","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"sn0s81444","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-eerg5","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"11. Put a cast iron dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"aznzl1446","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-81n3s","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"9jgfe1447","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bp0jp","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"12. After 30 minutes, very carefully remove the preheated dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"q9uk91449","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-8gfu","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ml9a21450","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-obar","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"13. Roll your loaf out of the basket and onto a piece of super handy parchment paper sprinkled with polenta or semolina flour and score as shown above or in the pattern of your choice.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"nkss01452","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6ggj4","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"j6vy51453","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-cds65","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"14. Remove the lid from the dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole dutch oven back into your oven.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"dsikc1455","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-18uuj","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"uqmfq1456","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-difv2","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 at this point and bake for 30 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40 minutes. Bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"a9z8y1458","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-40ikf","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mjlj91459","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-b082s","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Please let this cool at least an hour before slicing. It will make all the difference in the texture of this bread. If placed in a plastic bag after completely cooled the crust will soften and become more chewy.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:17.092Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:17.092Z","id":"f835037e-94ab-496d-a960-a046be8fa9ad"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"1. Add the flours, butter, salt and yeast to a stand mixer ( I don't sift) and mix on low speed with the hook attachment to somewhat combine.\n\n \n\n2. Add the molasses, honey and sourdough starter all at once and mix again on low speed to somewhat combine.\n\n \n\n3. Begin to drizzle in the liquid very slowly with the machine running on low speed until you have used a little more than 1 cup.\n\n \n\n4. Allow the machine to continue to work the dough until it begins to pull away from the sides of the mixer adding more liquid tablespoon by tablespoon if it seems too dry. If you go too fast and the dough gets too wet add more bread flour a few tablespoons at a time. The dough should become just slightly sticky and be very soft in about 5 minutes. Let the machine knead a few minutes after it comes together maybe 7-8 minutes total. The dough should be just tacky now and still very soft.\n\n \n\n5. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula. Flour your hands and then knead the dough just a few times by folding it over itself one direction and then the other.\n\n \n\n6. Gather the dough into a ball and place it in a large bowl that has a tablespoon of oil in it and roll it around so it's lightly covered with the oil.\n\n \n\n7. Cover the bowl with plastic wrap and set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!\n\n \n\n8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!\n\n \n\n 9. Roll it back out onto a floured board and degassed the dough by folding it over on itself 3-4 times, then started rolling it up like a newspaper fairly tightly. You want to get some decent surface tension going on the outside of the loaf you are forming.\n\n \n\n10. I put it in a floured proofing basket called a banneton to do its second rise. Cover it with the cover that often comes with your banneton or very loosely with plastic wrap that you've sprayed with oil.\n\n \n\nTip - You can make a proofing basket out of a bread basket or pan lined with towels but it's not nearly as convenient if you make bread regularly.\n\n \n\n11. Put a cast iron dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes. \n\n \n\n12. After 30 minutes, very carefully remove the preheated dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.\n\n \n\n13. Roll your loaf out of the basket and onto a piece of super handy parchment paper sprinkled with polenta or semolina flour and score as shown above or in the pattern of your choice.\n\n \n\n14. Remove the lid from the dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole dutch oven back into your oven. \n\n \n\n15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 at this point and bake for 30 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40 minutes. Bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom.\n\n \n\nPlease let this cool at least an hour before slicing. It will make all the difference in the texture of this bread. If placed in a plastic bag after completely cooled the crust will soften and become more chewy.","title":"Hazelnut Molasses Sourdough Bread"},{"introduction":"These muffins are so big and tasty! Filled with fresh blueberries from our garden, some maple syrup, hearty, crunchy cornmeal and the awesomeness of sourdough, these muffins are both filling and satisfying. ","servings":"6 jumbo muffins","photo":"wix:image://v1/577c92_e5f05b6ef89b45908d05ac76c251705